1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:03,182
Every day, banks process thousands of payments across countries,

2
00:00:03,222 --> 00:00:04,994
currencies and financial institutions.

3
00:00:05,034 --> 00:00:07,591
To manage these transactions efficiently, banks rely on powerful

4
00:00:07,631 --> 00:00:10,446
payment-processing platforms. One such platform is the Global Payments

5
00:00:10,486 --> 00:00:14,091
Platform, commonly known as GPP. Let's understand what it does and how it fits

6
00:00:14,131 --> 00:00:15,569
into the payment ecosystem.

7
00:00:16,821 --> 00:00:20,175
Let's look at where GPP fits. In our project context, an incoming payment

8
00:00:20,215 --> 00:00:22,594
instruction can travel from the sending bank through the SWIFT network and

9
00:00:22,634 --> 00:00:24,769
reach the Payments Management Gateway, or PMG.

10
00:00:24,809 --> 00:00:27,519
PMG acts as an integration point between SWIFT and the downstream

11
00:00:27,559 --> 00:00:30,148
payment-processing environment. Depending on the configured flow,

12
00:00:30,188 --> 00:00:33,132
the payment can be directed towards the legacy IPS engine or the newer GPP

13
00:00:33,172 --> 00:00:36,739
platform. The migration from IPS to GPP is intended to transition payment

14
00:00:36,779 --> 00:00:38,572
processing onto the target platform.

15
00:00:39,848 --> 00:00:42,849
Once the payment reaches GPP, the platform processes it through a series of

16
00:00:42,889 --> 00:00:45,557
steps. First, it receives the payment instruction.

17
00:00:45,597 --> 00:00:48,210
Next, it performs validation to check whether the message and its business

18
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:51,288
data meet the required criteria. Configured business rules are then applied to

19
00:00:51,328 --> 00:00:53,008
determine how the payment should be handled.

20
00:00:53,048 --> 00:00:55,646
The transaction is subsequently routed for further processing.

21
00:00:55,686 --> 00:00:58,750
Finally, the platform generates or updates payment statuses so that downstream

22
00:00:58,790 --> 00:01:01,132
systems and operational teams can track what happened.

23
00:01:02,398 --> 00:01:06,778
To understand GPP, it is also important to understand ISO 20022 messaging.

24
00:01:06,818 --> 00:01:09,754
These messages provide a structured way for financial institutions to exchange

25
00:01:09,794 --> 00:01:13,101
payment information. For example, pacs.008 is used for a financial

26
00:01:13,141 --> 00:01:16,480
institution-to-financial institution customer credit transfer, while pacs.009

27
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:19,407
represents a financial institution-to-financial institution credit transfer.

28
00:01:19,447 --> 00:01:23,512
pacs.002 communicates payment status, pacs.004 is used for payment returns,

29
00:01:23,552 --> 00:01:25,988
and camt.056 is a payment cancellation request.

30
00:01:26,028 --> 00:01:28,528
Understanding the purpose of each message helps a Business Analyst trace

31
00:01:28,568 --> 00:01:30,659
payment journeys and investigate exceptions.

32
00:01:31,919 --> 00:01:34,512
Another important concept is Nostro and Vostro accounts.

33
00:01:34,552 --> 00:01:37,225
A Nostro account is our bank's account maintained with another bank.

34
00:01:37,265 --> 00:01:39,741
A Vostro account is another bank's account maintained with us.

35
00:01:39,781 --> 00:01:42,281
These accounts are important in correspondent banking and cross-border

36
00:01:42,321 --> 00:01:45,031
settlement. Remember that sending a payment instruction and settling or

37
00:01:45,071 --> 00:01:48,235
posting the funds are related, but they are not necessarily the same event.

38
00:01:49,501 --> 00:01:52,615
But not every payment is successful. A transaction may fail validation or

39
00:01:52,655 --> 00:01:55,841
encounter a processing exception. In such cases, the platform and surrounding

40
00:01:55,881 --> 00:01:59,470
processes may generate a rejection status, such as a pacs.002, depending on

41
00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:02,857
the applicable flow. This is where operational procedures become important.

42
00:02:02,897 --> 00:02:05,107
A Business Analyst needs to understand what triggered the failure,

43
00:02:05,147 --> 00:02:07,744
which system generated the status, what downstream impact occurred,

44
00:02:07,784 --> 00:02:09,940
and how the exception is investigated and resolved.

45
00:02:11,201 --> 00:02:14,384
To become effective in payments analysis, don't just memorise message names.

46
00:02:14,424 --> 00:02:17,409
Understand the complete payment journey, how systems interact, how statuses

47
00:02:17,449 --> 00:02:19,465
are generated and what happens when something goes wrong.

48
00:02:19,505 --> 00:02:22,207
That is the foundation for understanding GPP from a Business Analyst's

49
00:02:22,247 --> 00:02:23,177
perspective.
